  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════
       SLOT: what-is
  ════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <Fragment slot="what-is">
    <p>
      A <strong>calorie deficit planner</strong> is a customized, timeline-driven weight loss planner.
      Rather than giving you a single static daily target, this planner computes the exact thermodynamic
      deficit required to move from your current weight to your goal weight over a specified number of weeks.
      It then plots out a week-by-week roadmap, showing you what your weight should look like each week if
      you consistently maintain the deficit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Creating a structured timeline is essential for successful weight loss. Multiple behavioral studies
      demonstrate that individuals who set structured, weekly milestones are significantly more likely to maintain
      consistency and successfully retain muscle mass during a fat loss cut. This is because a planner sets realistic
      expectations: instead of expecting instant overnight results, you target a healthy, progressive fat loss rate
      of 0.5% to 1% of body weight per week.
    </p>
    <p>
      By planning your weight loss over a 12-week timeline, you allow your body to adjust naturally. Short-term,
      extreme crash diets almost always fail because they force the body to burn valuable lean muscle mass and
      downregulate the thyroid, triggering severe hunger. A moderate, calculated calorie deficit provides sufficient
      nutrients to support training intensity and recovery while forcing your body to steadily burn stored fat for energy.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════
       SLOT: how-to
  ════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <Fragment slot="how-to">
    <p>
      The planner calculates weight loss by matching total required weight change to metabolic energy values:
    </p>

    <div class="formula-block" role="math" aria-label="Calorie deficit planner calculations">
      <p class="formula-label">The Weight Loss Planning Math</p>
      <code class="formula-code">1. Compute Total Weight to Lose
   Total Loss (kg) = Current Weight − Goal Weight

2. Determine Weekly Loss Rate
   Weekly Loss (kg) = Total Loss ÷ Timeline (Weeks)

3. Convert Fat Mass to Calorie Equivalent (1 kg fat ≈ 7,700 kcal)
   Weekly Calorie Deficit = Weekly Loss × 7,700
   Daily Calorie Deficit = Weekly Calorie Deficit ÷ 7

4. Calculate Target Intake
   Daily Calorie Target = TDEE − Daily Calorie Deficit</code>
      <p class="formula-source">
        Source: Scientific consensus parameters using the Mifflin-St Jeor TDEE baseline.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Step-by-Step Example</h3>
    <p>
      Let's look at an individual who weighs 80 kg, has a goal weight of 74 kg, a timeline of 12 weeks,
      and a maintenance TDEE of 2,400 kcal:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Total Loss:</strong> 80 kg − 74 kg = 6 kg of weight to lose.</li>
      <li><strong>Weekly Target:</strong> 6 kg ÷ 12 weeks = 0.5 kg of weight loss per week.</li>
      <li><strong>Daily Deficit:</strong> (0.5 kg × 7,700) ÷ 7 = 550 kcal deficit per day.</li>
      <li><strong>Target Calorie Intake:</strong> TDEE (2,400) − Deficit (550) = 1,850 kcal/day.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════
       SLOT: ranges
  ════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <Fragment slot="ranges">
    <p>
      Setting a safe rate of fat loss is critical to protect your muscle mass, hormones, and general health.
    </p>

    <h3>Evaluations of Weight Loss Deficit Rates</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Daily Deficit Size</th>
          <th>Weekly Weight Loss Target</th>
          <th>Deficit Sustainability</th>
          <th>Physical / Metabolic Profile</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>250 calories</strong> (Mild Deficit)</td>
          <td>~0.25 kg / week</td>
          <td>Very High</td>
          <td>Excellent for preserving muscle mass. Highly recommended for athletes.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>500 calories</strong> (Moderate Deficit)</td>
          <td>~0.5 kg / week</td>
          <td>High</td>
          <td>The clinical gold standard for safe, steady, sustainable fat loss.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>750 calories</strong> (Large Deficit)</td>
          <td>~0.75 kg / week</td>
          <td>Moderate</td>
          <td>Acceptable only for individuals with significant initial body fat.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>1,000+ calories</strong> (Aggressive Deficit)</td>
          <td>~1.0+ kg / week</td>
          <td>Low</td>
          <td>High risk of muscle wasting and metabolic adaptive slowdown. Not recommended.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Adjusting for Safety Thresholds</h3>
    <p>
      If your target calorie budget drops below the biological safety threshold of **1,200 calories (women)** or
      **1,500 calories (men)**, our tool will display a safety warning. In this scenario, do not force a larger
      deficit. Instead, extend your timeline (e.g. from 12 weeks to 16 weeks) or increase your physical activity
      (raising your TDEE) to achieve your weight goal safely without starving your body of essential nutrients.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

</CalculatorLayout>

